{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CggDkTsMiBkpbk3vBBtw7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I checked what tools are available in this session. The only connected server is **Galaxy** (bioinformatics workflow execution: histories, datasets, Galaxy tools, IWC workflows, pages). There is no ClinicalTrials.gov tool, no PubMed/NCBI tool, and no web access, so **I cannot query the trial registry for you.** The answer below is from my own knowledge, with a training cutoff of May 2026.\n\nTwo important caveats before the content:\n\n- **Recruitment status is exactly the thing that goes stale.** \"Active\" is a live field on ClinicalTrials.gov; anything I state about it is a snapshot, not a lookup. Please re-check on clinicaltrials.gov using the search terms `KRAS G12C` with status filters.\n- **NCT numbers below are recalled from memory, not retrieved.** Treat them as search hints to verify, not as verified identifiers. The trial *program names* (CodeBreaK, KRYSTAL, KRASCENDO, SUNRAY) are more reliable search handles than the digits.\n\n## Approved agents with ongoing trial programs\n\n**Sotorasib (AMG 510, Lumakras/Lumykras — Amgen)** — FDA accelerated approval May 2021 for previously treated *KRAS* G12C NSCLC; January 2025 approval of sotorasib + panitumumab for chemorefractory *KRAS* G12C colorectal cancer. The CodeBreaK program remains active and includes CodeBreaK 100 (phase 1/2, NCT03600883), CodeBreaK 200 (phase 3 vs docetaxel, NCT04303780), CodeBreaK 300 (sotorasib + panitumumab in CRC, NCT05198934), and CodeBreaK 201/202 (first-line NSCLC combinations).\n\n**Adagrasib (MRTX849, Krazati — Mirati/Bristol Myers Squibb)** — FDA accelerated approval December 2022 for NSCLC; June 2024 accelerated approval of adagrasib + cetuximab for *KRAS* G12C CRC. KRYSTAL program: KRYSTAL-1 (NCT03785249), KRYSTAL-12 (phase 3 vs docetaxel, NCT04685135), KRYSTAL-7 (adagrasib + pembrolizumab in first-line NSCLC, NCT04613596), KRYSTAL-10 (adagrasib + cetuximab, second-line CRC, NCT04793958).\n\n## Investigational agents in active development\n\n- **Divarasib (GDC-6036, Genentech/Roche)** — phase 1 NCT04449874; phase 3 KRASCENDO program in NSCLC and CRC, including a head-to-head against sotorasib/adagrasib.\n- **Olomorasib (LY3537982, Eli Lilly)** — phase 1 NCT04956640; phase 3 SUNRAY-01 in first-line NSCLC combined with pembrolizumab.\n- **Elironrasib (RMC-6291, Revolution Medicines)** — RAS(ON) tri-complex inhibitor, studied alone and with daraxonrasib (RMC-6236, RAS(ON) multi-selective).\n- **MK-1084 (Merck)** — phase 1 plus a first-line NSCLC combination with pembrolizumab.\n- **Opnurasib (JDQ443, Novartis)** — KontRASt program.\n- **BI 1823911 (Boehringer Ingelheim)** — including combination with the SOS1 inhibitor BI 1701963.\n- **China-approved/China-developed**: fulzerasib (GFH925/IBI351), glecirasib (JAB-21822, Jacobio), garsorasib (D-1553, InventisBio), D3S-001 (D3 Bio).\n\nThe dominant current theme is movement from later-line monotherapy into **first-line combinations** (with anti-PD-1, with EGFR antibodies in CRC, and with SHP2/SOS1 inhibitors to blunt adaptive resistance).\n\n## ANSWER\n\nYes — the KRAS G12C inhibitor space has many active trials, but **I could not verify this with a tool**: this session has only Galaxy bioinformatics tools, no clinical-trial registry or web access, so the following is from my knowledge (cutoff May 2026) and the NCT numbers are recalled, not retrieved.\n\n- **Approved, with ongoing trials**: sotorasib (CodeBreaK 100/200/300/201/202) and adagrasib (KRYSTAL-1/-7/-10/-12).\n- **Investigational**: divarasib (KRASCENDO), olomorasib (SUNRAY-01), elironrasib/RMC-6291, MK-1084, opnurasib/JDQ443, BI 1823911, plus fulzerasib, glecirasib, garsorasib and D3S-001.\n\nVerify current recruitment status at clinicaltrials.gov searching `KRAS G12C`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":